[Thinking]
**Suggesting appsettings fix**

I need to recommend a fix to the appsettings by changing it to only include a list of origins without the asterisk. It's also essential to ensure that the WithOrigins call uses the Split and Trim methods, along with RemovePostFix. I can provide a final code example to illustrate this. That way, it’s clear how to implement the changes for better functionality and security. Let’s make sure it’s done right!
[/Thinking]

Summary: The browser says "Access-Control-Allow-Origin missing" because your CORS policy is never applied to the responses (or the policy is invalid). Fixes are one of these: register a valid list of origins (don't pass "*" into WithOrigins), ensure app.UseCors(DefaultCorsPolicyName) runs before authentication/endpoint handling so preflight gets handled, and/or use AllowAnyOrigin() if you really want "*". Below are concrete checks and recommended changes.

Troubleshooting checklist (quick):
1. Confirm the policy is actually being used in the pipeline:
   - You must call app.UseCors(DefaultCorsPolicyName) in Configure(...) before authentication and before endpoints.
2. Do not pass "*" into WithOrigins:
   - WithOrigins accepts explicit origins, not "*". Passing "*" as an origin will make the policy invalid and no header will be emitted.
   - If you want to allow any origin, call .AllowAnyOrigin() (but that cannot be combined with .AllowCredentials()).
3. Ensure preflight (OPTIONS) is handled and returns CORS headers:
   - Browser sends OPTIONS for many GET/POST calls when headers/cors rules apply. Make sure the server returns the CORS headers to OPTIONS requests.
4. If you require credentials (cookies/auth header), call .AllowCredentials() and use explicit origins (no AllowAnyOrigin).
5. Verify with browser devtools the OPTIONS and the actual request responses — check whether Access-Control-Allow-Origin is present.

Concrete fixes and example code

1) Fix appsettings.json
- Remove the "*" from the CorsOrigins entry. If you want to allow the Angular dev server only:

"App": {
  "ServerRootAddress": "http://localhost:21023/",
  "ClientRootAddress": "http://localhost:4200/",
  "CorsOrigins": "http://localhost:4200"
}

(Ensure no trailing slash or that RemovePostFix("/") normalizes it.)

2) Configure CORS services (services.Configure / SensorAppService.cs or Startup.cs)
Your snippet is almost fine but make sure the origin list is correct and trimming is applied:

// register CORS policy
services.AddCors(options =>
{
    options.AddPolicy(DefaultCorsPolicyName, builder =>
    {
        var origins = _appConfiguration["App:CorsOrigins"]
            .Split(',', StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries)
            .Select(o => o.Trim().RemovePostFix("/")) // remove trailing slash if your helper does that
            .ToArray();

        builder
            .WithOrigins(origins)
            .AllowAnyHeader()
            .AllowAnyMethod();
            // If you need credentials (cookies), also add: .AllowCredentials();
    });
});

3) Ensure middleware runs (Startup.Configure)
Make sure you call UseCors with the same policy name and that it runs before UseAuthentication/UseAuthorization and before endpoints:

public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
{
    app.UseRouting();

    // IMPORTANT: CORS must be before authentication and endpoints
    app.UseCors(DefaultCorsPolicyName);

    app.UseAuthentication();
    app.UseAuthorization();

    app.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
    {
        endpoints.MapControllers();
        // or endpoints.MapDefaultControllerRoute();
    });
}

If you use endpoint routing and prefer to apply policy per controller, you can also:
endpoints.MapControllers().RequireCors(DefaultCorsPolicyName);

4) If you want wildcard (allow any origin)
- Replace WithOrigins(...) with .AllowAnyOrigin() (and remove AllowCredentials()):

builder.AllowAnyOrigin()
       .AllowAnyHeader()
       .AllowAnyMethod();

5) Confirm preflight handling
- In Network tab filter for OPTIONS to your endpoint. If response to OPTIONS lacks Access-Control-Allow-Origin then CORS middleware isn't running or is misconfigured.
- If controller/action has custom middleware interfering with OPTIONS (or you're returning 401/302 etc), that can block CORS headers. Make sure CORS middleware runs early.

6) Authorization + CORS
- If anonymous preflight (OPTIONS) is returned as 401 because your pipeline enforces [Authorize] before CORS middleware, you must let CORS middleware run first so that the preflight succeeds. That's why app.UseCors must be before app.UseAuthentication().

7) Example minimal test
- After changes, restart app, open DevTools -> Network -> do a GET from Angular. Inspect response headers; you should see:
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://localhost:4200

If you still see nothing:
- Check that the appConfiguration value is what you expect at runtime (log it).
- Ensure RemovePostFix("/") doesn't produce empty string or other unexpected origin.
- Try hardcoding WithOrigins("http://localhost:4200") to isolate the issue.

Common gotchas that match your symptoms
- appsettings had "http://localhost:4200/,*" so WithOrigins receives "*" which is not valid — policy may silently fail and no header is emitted.
- CORS middleware placed after authentication/authorization so OPTIONS preflight is rejected with 401 or no CORS header.
- Using .AllowCredentials() with .AllowAnyOrigin() — that will fail and not set header (you must use explicit origins if AllowCredentials is used).

If you want, paste:
- the Startup.Configure method (or Program.cs pipeline) and
- the actual value logged from _appConfiguration["App:CorsOrigins"]
and I will give an exact patch.

